# Git workflow

## Create or checkout a branch
- Start new work from an up-to-date `main`:
  ```bash
  git checkout main
  git pull origin main
  git checkout -b feat/<short-name>   # or fix/<short-name> for bugs
  ```
- Branch names: `feat/<name>` for features, `fix/<name>` for bugs.
  Examples: `feat/garmin-connector`, `fix/default-karvonen-hr-method`.
- To switch to an existing branch: `git checkout <branch>`.
- Run `git status` first. Never switch branches with uncommitted changes that do not belong to the task.

## Commit (only when the user asks)
- Stage only the files for the requested change: `git add <files>`. Avoid `git add -A` unless asked.
- Check the staged content with `git diff --staged` before committing. Never commit secrets or `.env` files.
- Message style: Bullet points, concise
- Describe what the code does. Never mention the assistant, Cursor, AI, "I" or "you" in the message.
- Author: use the git config identity (`grepMirror mroy0407@gmail.com`).
  Never add a `Co-authored-by: Cursor` trailer or any other AI attribution.
- Pass the message through a heredoc so the formatting is kept:
  ```bash
  git commit -F - <<'EOF'
  feat: Add Garmin connector to import activities

  - Parse FIT files from the Garmin export
  - Tag imported activities with source "garmin"
  EOF
  ```
- Do not amend, rebase or force-push unless the user explicitly asks.

## Push (only when the user asks)
- First push of a new branch: `git push -u origin <branch>`.
- Later pushes: `git push`.
- Confirm with `git status -sb`: the branch should no longer show `ahead`.
- Do not force-push unless the user explicitly asks.
